description:
Problem: Several enterprise customers use legacy SDKs that assume cursor semantics and error codes of the historical embeddings/rerank endpoints. We recently shipped a unified embeddings + rerank API that changed cursor shapes (opaque JSON -> compact base64 token), introduced tombstones for deleted vectors, and refined HTTP error codes for fine-grained retry behavior. These changes break third-party SDKs and some internal agents that expect the old contract.

Goal: Build a compatibility adapter layer that sits in the API gateway and provides a translation surface so legacy clients keep working without code changes. The adapter should: (1) present stable, opaque cursors that remain valid across server-side changes, (2) translate new error semantics to legacy-friendly HTTP codes and idempotency hints, (3) preserve streaming and tool-calling behavior for rerank flows where applicable, and (4) be toggleable with feature flags for incremental rollout.

Success criteria: 
- No increase in customer-facing 4xx/5xx errors for legacy SDK traffic during canary and full rollout;
- End-to-end iterator tests (pagination + deletes) pass against adapter for 99.9% of synthetic workloads;
- Backcompat test suite added to CI and run on every runtime release;
- Migration guide published and communicated to large customers with custom SDKs.
